<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Band-e Amir (dam)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · Also known as Band-e Amir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Band-e <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> is a dam on the Kur River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, built in the 10th century for the Buyid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a>. Its masonry weir raised the river to irrigate the surrounding plain and drove water-mills. Admired by medieval geographers, it remained in use for centuries and gave its name to the stretch of river below it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/band-e-amir-dam.jpg" alt="Band-e Amir (dam)" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eugène Flandin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Band_Amir_by_Eug%C3%A8ne_Flandin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
